data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`** — it's already safe in that regard. It uses `Thread.Start`/`Join`, which still work in .NET 8.

However, the pattern is outdated. Here's the modern .NET 8 equivalent using `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task`:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
        {
            item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
        });
    }
}
```

**If you need cancellation** (the actual replacement for `Thread.Abort`):

```csharp
public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct)
{
    await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, ct, async (item, token) =>
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

**Key migration points for `Thread.Abort` in general:**
| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation |
| `ThreadAbortException` | `OperationCanceledException` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Not needed — cancellation is cooperative |

`Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The fix is always cooperative cancellation via `CancellationTokenSource`.
